findings.body:
`prepare_tools` returns `crate::Result<PathBuf>`, but `write_and_make_executable` now returns `Result<(), FileWriteError>`. The `?` operator needs `impl From<FileWriteError> for crate::Error`, and that impl doesn't exist. I grepped `crates/tauri-bundler/src/error.rs`: the only conversions are `#[from]` variants for `io::Error`, `anyhow::Error` and similar, and nothing else in the repo references `FileWriteError`.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/bundle/linux/appimage/mod.rs" lines="284-316"}

The custom error type also doesn't add much. `io::Error` already carries the OS error, and the variant name only tells you which of two adjacent calls failed. The simplest fix is to go back to `std::io::Result<()>`, which `?` converts into `crate::Error::IoError`.

::suggestion{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/bundle/linux/appimage/mod.rs" lines="310-316"}
fn write_and_make_executable(path: &Path, data: &[u8]) -> std::io::Result<()> {
  use std::os::unix::fs::PermissionsExt;

  fs::write(path, data)?;
  fs::set_permissions(path, fs::Permissions::from_mode(0o770))?;

  Ok(())
}

If you really want the richer error context, add a `#[from]` variant to `crate::Error` (or map the error to `crate::Error::GenericError`) rather than defining a private error type the caller can't propagate.

The order of the two calls was swapped. `fs::set_permissions` on a path that doesn't exist fails with `NotFound`, and `write_and_make_executable` is only ever called when the file is missing (`if !x.exists()`). So on a clean machine every download or embedded write fails before any bytes reach disk, and AppImage bundling can't bootstrap its tools at all.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/bundle/linux/appimage/mod.rs" lines="310-316"}

::callout{type="warning"}
There's a second problem with this order: if the file did exist, `fs::write` truncates and rewrites it, so the permissions step would only work by accident. Write first, then set the mode, as the original code did.

::suggestion{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/bundle/linux/appimage/mod.rs" lines="313-314"}
  fs::write(path, data)?;
  fs::set_permissions(path, fs::Permissions::from_mode(0o770))?;

The GTK block now runs when the file **exists** and skips it when it's missing. That's the opposite of every other artifact in `prepare_tools`, including the GStreamer block right below it:

::diff{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/bundle/linux/appimage/mod.rs" lines="242-252"}

On a clean CI runner or a new developer machine, `linuxdeploy-plugin-gtk.sh` never gets created, and `linuxdeploy --plugin gtk` fails because it can't find the plugin. On machines with a cached copy, the script gets rewritten on every build, which is harmless but clearly not intended.

::suggestion{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/bundle/linux/appimage/mod.rs" lines="243-243"}
  if !gtk.exists() {

The release asset for 32-bit x86 is published as `linuxdeploy-i383.AppImage`, which is why `linuxdeploy_arch` exists and why the function comment says "keep linuxdeploy_arch contained". This PR changes the URL to interpolate `{arch}`, so i686 builds request `linuxdeploy-i686.AppImage`. That doesn't match the cached filename, and it's likely an asset that doesn't exist, so the download fails.

::diff{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/bundle/linux/appimage/mod.rs" lines="235-240"}

This change has nothing to do with inlining the plugin scripts and looks accidental.

::suggestion{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/bundle/linux/appimage/mod.rs" lines="238-238"}
    let data = download(&format!("https://github.com/tauri-apps/binary-releases/releases/download/linuxdeploy/linuxdeploy-{linuxdeploy_arch}.AppImage"))?;

Once the existence check is fixed, users who already have `linuxdeploy-plugin-gtk.sh` or `linuxdeploy-plugin-gstreamer.sh` in their tools cache will keep using the previously downloaded copy forever. Later fixes to the embedded scripts will never reach them.

::code{file="crates/tauri-bundler/src/bundle/linux/appimage/mod.rs" lines="242-252"}

The scripts are now embedded, so writing them costs nothing (a few KB with no network I/O). Consider writing them unconditionally on every run, or only when the on-disk contents differ from the embedded bytes. Then the bundled version is always the one that runs.
